  "notes": "The maximal country power-ballad trio. The tracking date is documented in player counts, not adjectives — \"seven players cutting together (two guitarists, drums, bass, scratch vocal, grand piano, synth) in a room chosen specifically for its drum sound\" — which is why the humanize is a live-band reading rather than a grid one. The drum grammar is quoted: \"hats-and-kick verse, snare from the chorus, tom-driven bridge, final chorus with doubled snare and continuous crash.\" Harmony is \"I–V–vi–IV and vi–IV–I–V; the minor cuts use i–VI–III–VII,\" and the minor family matters here because the entry flags \"'What Hurts the Most' F minor — one of the few minor-tonic radio ballads in this lane.\" The bass is written by a band member who also produces: \"root motion with pop-style anticipations, 8ths under choruses,\" hence a real anticipation figure over high root-following. Melody is \"high-tenor, wide-range and melisma-heavy — the most ornamented male vocal in the section,\" which is the widest male register and the highest octave-jump probability in this batch. ⚠ Confidence is medium (research: high-medium) and the per-track key/tempo readings are [LOW-CONF]; the entry notes songdata's 132–172 cluster folds onto a 66–86 ballad spine. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the humanize values, the perc lane density and the counter ratio.",
